{"name":"Bluebell","hex":"#333399","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bluebell","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Various flowering plants with blue, usually pendulous, flowers.\n\nIn genus Hyacinthoides\ncommon bluebell (Hyacinthoides non-scripta)\nSpanish bluebell (Hyacinthoides hispanica)\nItalian bluebell (Hyacinthoides italica)\nIn other genera\ngrape hyacinth (Muscari)\n Virginia bluebell (Mertensia virginica)\nScottish bluebell (harebell) (Campanula rotundifolia)\n\nAustralian royal bluebell (Wahlenbergia gloriosa)\nTexas bluebell (Eustoma russellianum)\ndesert bluebells or California bluebell (Phacelia campanularia)","In genus Hyacinthoides\ncommon bluebell (Hyacinthoides non-scripta)\nSpanish bluebell (Hyacinthoides hispanica)\nItalian bluebell (Hyacinthoides italica)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bluebell"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hyacinthoides","blocks":[{"text":"Hyacinthoides is a genus of flowering plants in the family Asparagaceae, known as bluebells."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/2401_Bluebells.jpg/330px-2401_Bluebells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyacinthoides","wikidata":"Q159311"},{"title":"Hyacinthoides non-scripta","blocks":[{"text":"Hyacinthoides non-scripta is a bulbous perennial plant found in Atlantic areas from the north-western part of the Iberian Peninsula to the British Isles, and also frequently used as a garden plant. It is known in English as the common bluebell, English bluebell or simply bluebell, a name which is used in Scotland to refer to the harebell, Campanula rotundifolia. In spring, H. non-scripta produces a nodding, one-sided inflorescence of 5–12 tubular, sweet-scented violet–blue flowers, with strongly recurved tepals, and 3–6 long, linear, basal leaves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Hyacinthoides_non-scripta_%28Common_Bluebell%29.jpg/330px-Hyacinthoides_non-scripta_%28Common_Bluebell%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyacinthoides_non-scripta","wikidata":"Q164013"},{"title":"Blue-gray","blocks":[{"text":"Blue-gray is a medium blue-gray color on the cool side of the color wheel. The name blue-gray was introduced by Crayola for a crayon color used from 1958 to 1990. The complementary color is #CC9966, a warm khaki or brown."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue-gray","wikidata":"Q4928721"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Hyacinthoides_in_Narrow_Wood%2C_Wadborough_-_geograph.org.uk_-_781186.jpg/330px-Hyacinthoides_in_Narrow_Wood%2C_Wadborough_-_geograph.org.uk_-_781186.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Hyacinthoides_in_Narrow_Wood%2C_Wadborough_-_geograph.org.uk_-_781186.jpg/330px-Hyacinthoides_in_Narrow_Wood%2C_Wadborough_-_geograph.org.uk_-_781186.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHyacinthoides_in_Narrow_Wood%2C_Wadborough_-_geograph.org.uk_-_781186.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Hyacinthoides_hispanica_pm.jpg/500px-Hyacinthoides_hispanica_pm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Hyacinthoides_hispanica_pm.jpg/500px-Hyacinthoides_hispanica_pm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHyacinthoides_hispanica_pm.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Pryor%27s_Wood_Bluebells_2017-04-22-7.jpg/500px-Pryor%27s_Wood_Bluebells_2017-04-22-7.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser